{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life and Family* Raymond's early life was marked by the assassination of his father in 1152. His mother Hodierna took him and his sister Melisende to the court of their aunt Melisende Queen of Jerusalem. Raymond's mother then returned to Tripoli and took up the regency on behalf of her son.\n\nAfter the death of his mother in 1164 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1174 after spending about 10 years in captivity. Upon his release Raymond returned to Tripoli and took up the countship. *Reign and Military Campaigns* Raymond's reign was marked by a series of conflicts with the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. In 1175 he allied himself with the Assassins against Saladin. Raymond also fought against Saladin's forces in the Battle of Barqik in 1176 but was defeated.\n\nIn 1180 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures Princess of Galilee. This marriage was an attempt to strengthen his ties with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. However it also led to a rift between Raymond and Guy of Lusignan who was the husband of Sibylla of Jerusalem and a rival claimant to the throne. *The Crusader Politics and the Precursor to Hattin* Raymond's actions were motivated by his desire to protect his county from the growing power of Saladin. However his conflicts with Guy of Lusignan and other nobles ultimately contributed to the downfall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. The divisions within the Crusader leadership were evident at the Council of Tiberias in 1187 where Raymond's advice was ignored. The consequences of this were disastrous and the Crusader army was defeated at the Battle of Hattin later that year. Ray